## 1. Introduction
The booming demand for energy storage and conversion to address the challenges presented by growing population has led to a significant increase in the utilization of fossil fuels, which was proved to display shortcomings of being depleted fast and environmentally unfriendly.[1] As a result, some better gas, solid and liquid waste managements, greener sustainable energy sources, and efficient energy conversion technologies are crucially needed.[2] Over the past decades, a large amount of research efforts, both in academia and industry, have been devoted to searching for advanced scientific knowledge related to renewable energy sources, such as hydroelectricity, wind, sunlight, biofuels, geothermal heat, and so on.[3,4] However, none of them is constant or reliable because of their great dependence on the weather conditions, these energy sources are also unpredictable and may be subject to fluctuations in of S-doped carbon materials for a wider detection of DA, UA (uric acid), and AA (ascorbic acid).[23,24] The advantages of easy preparing, nontoxic and excellent electrical conductivity of carbonaceous materials, which are rare among energy storage materials, make carbonaceous materials superior to most of the energy storage materials.[25–27]
There are varieties of approaches for the preparation of carbon materials, such as directly carbonizing from organic precursors, physically or chemically carbonizing from carbon, template methods using zeolites and mesoporous silica, solvothermal and hydrothermal methods with elevated temperature, the electrical arc methods, and chemical vapor decomposition (CVD) methods.[28–34] Among all these approaches, directly carbonizing from organic precursors is the most frequently used method to prepare nanoporous carbons (NPCs) due to its flexibility and simplicity.[35–37] However, these NPC materials present certain drawbacks, such as low surface areas, disordered structures, and ununiformed sizes, which will greatly limit their applications.[25] As studies have progressed, researchers found that carbon materials derived from metalorganic frameworks (MOFs) could overcome these limitations.
Metal–organic frameworks, which are also named porous coordination polymers (PCPs), are crystalline porous materials with periodic network structures formed by metal ions (or metal clusters) and organic ligands.[38–42] They are usually prepared by solvothermal methods and used as precursors or templates to form nanostructured materials.[43–45] So far, many researchers have highlighted the advantages of MOFs. For example, in 2012, Sumida et  al.[46] summarized the MOFs as potential new solid adsorbents within $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture systems, and various performance parameters of the MOFs were given in the context. Taking advantage of high surface area, MOFs show great adsorption activity for many gases or mixtures. Meanwhile, thanks to the controllable pore size, a high selectivity for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ over the other components of the gas mixture can be achieved. In that review, they summarized the main issues for the gas separations relevant to precombustion $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture, postcombustion $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture, and oxy-fuel combustion for the research of next-generation energy-saving materials. In addition, in 2012, Lin et  al.[47] reviewed the MOFs for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ storage. Particularly, they focused on the methods of preparing MOFs. The diversity of the synthetic method results in the diversity of MOF material structure, which further affects its possible application. The key to improve the adsorption capacity is to enhance the interaction between gas and the frameworks, which means a better synthetic method should be developed to get a more suitable structure for gas separation.
MOF-derived carbon materials maintain various advantages originating from their MOFs, such as high porosity (up to $90\%$ ), extremely high surface area (almost $10\ 000\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1},$ ), tunable pore sizes, outstanding cycle lifetime, and so on.[47,48] As a result, MOF-derived carbon materials have shown promising applications including adsorption, energy storage and conversion, gas storage and separation, catalysis, chemical sensing, and solid phase extraction.[49–52] Heretofore, many outstanding explorations have been devoted to improving performance of these applications. Ma et al.[53] designed a hierarchically porous nitrogen-doped carbon using unique $Z\mathrm{n}$ -based MOF (noted as MOF-5) as the template and nitrogen source. By changing the temperature, the pore volume of the resultant was easily tuned and the sample obtained at a temperature of $900~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ exhibited a highest $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity of $3.70\mathrm{\mmol\g^{-1}}$ at $0~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (1 atm). Moreover, Wang et al.[54] presented a novel strategy to transform MOFs with no catalytic activity into carbon-based materials and demonstrated high storage capacity and stability. This approach was developed by utilizing MOF-guest precursors, and because of the unique guest impregnation, all the porous MOFs that can replace the guests can be potentially used as the precursors, thus extending its application. Dong et  al.[55] prepared three MOFs, Fe-MOF- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , $Z{\mathrm{r-MOF}}.$ - $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{La}{\cdot}\mathrm{MOF}{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , by the solvothermal method and then converted them to the corresponding carbon nanocomposites after pyrolysis. The sample which performed best among three samples is La-MOF- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ -derived biosensor.
In this review, we have summarized the applications of MOF-derived carbon materials, and a brief generalization is shown in Scheme 1. We focused on the applications in batteries including lithium-ion batteries (LIBs), lithium–sulfur batteries (LSB), sodium-ion batteries (SIBs), and other kinds of batteries such as lithium–selenium (Li–Se) batteries, supercapacitors (SCs) in terms of metal oxide/carbon (C) composites and metal sulfide/carbon (C) composites, electrocatalytic reactions including oxygen reduction reactions (ORR), oxygen evolution reactions (OER) and hydrogen evolution reactions (HER), water treatment, and so on. To close, here, some opinions are given about the future developments in this fascinating research field.
Huan Pang received his Ph.D. degree from Nanjing University in 2011. He is now a university distinguished professor in Yangzhou University. In the past 10 years, his group has been engaged in the design and synthesis of functional nanomaterials, especially for MOFbased materials. His research interests include the development of inorganic nanostructures and their applications in nano-electrochemistry with a focus on energy devices.
## 2. Batteries
As the common energy storage devices, rechargeable batteries have lots of advantages involving environmental friendly, high cycle stability, high storage efficiency, low maintenance cost, and so on, and because of these, LIBs have been widely used in electronics.[56,57] As the energy consumes fast, LIBs have become the most promising energy storage systems (EES) for green energy. However, some shortcomings are gradually coming out, such as highly costly materials, safety issues, low power density, and energy density. These all depended on the quality of the electrode materials.[58,59] So as to efficiently improve the electrochemical activities of batteries, the carbon materials derived from MOFs were recently designed.[60] As is known to all, controlling morphology of electrode materials in fabrication process is of great significance to the chemical performance of a battery, so we list out these applications in different kinds of batteries according to their morphology of carbonaceous materials in electrode. The electrode materials involving in this review are listed in Table 1.
## 2.1. LIBs
## 2.1.1. Normal Porous Carbon
With the emergence of portable electronic devices and electric vehicles, LIBs with long cycle lives as well as fast charging/ discharging rates are quite necessary. Normal porous carbon is one of the most promising alternatives to traditional graphite materials.[110] Besides, titanium dioxide $\left(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\right)$ , in view of its material features, might be a probable anode candidate for fast rechargeable LIBs due to its low volume change during cycling and high rate capability. To combine their advantages together, Wang et  al.[61] demonstrated a novel two-step heat-treatment method to prepare $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}$ composite to be an LIB anode material while taking the Ti-MOF (MIL-125(Ti)) as the precursor. When the current density was $0.10\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ , the resulting $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}$ displayed a superior rate capability and satisfactory reversible capacity of almost $400\ \mathrm{mA}\mathrm{~h~g^{-1}~}$ . Otherwise, Xiu et  al.[62] fabricated ultrafine TiN particles embedded homogeneously in a porous N-doped carbon matrix and performed remarkable lithium storage capabilities as well. However, considering the cost of electrode materials, researchers demand a low-cost material for a wide spread application. A material contains lithium iron phosphate $\mathrm{(LiFePO_{4})}$ ) nanoparticles and nitrogendoped carbon networks (LFP/N-CNWs) would be appropriate for LIBs owing to its reasonable price, desirable theoretical discharge capacity $(\approx170\ \mathrm{mA}\mathrm{\h\g}^{-1})$ ), and environmental benignity according to Liu et al.’s exploration.[63,111,112] The porous LFP/NCNW composites used a unique Fe-based MOF (MIL-100) to serve as sources of Fe and C and the template of the final composite. The synthesis procedure for LFP/N-CNWs is clearly shown in Figure 1b. A highly porous structure can be seen in Figure 1f. In the merits of the porous structure, which owned a surface area of $129{\mathrm{m}}^{2}{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ , the $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ diffusion coefficient can be significantly enhanced. Then, extremely high volumetric specific capacities were successfully achieved for the LFP/N-CNWs materials (as shown in Figure 1c). A discharge capacity of $93.60\mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ was still exhibited even at a current rate of $20\mathrm{C}$ ( $.\mathrm{~C~}=~170~\mathrm{\mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1})$ ), which may attribute to the strong connection between ${\mathrm{LiFePO}}_{4}$ nanoparticles and the nitrogendoped carbon networks. Other nanoporous carbon with desired structure and electrochemical performances may be achieved via a facile method. Early in 2015, with the assistance of a zeolitic imidazolate framework (ZIF), a porous carbon–coated $\mathrm{CuCo}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ concave polyhedron (noted as CCP, as shown in Figure 1e) was successfully prepared by Ma et al.[64] In this work, the ZIF not only played a role as the template for the polyhedron formation, but also provided a carbon matrix to coat $\mathrm{CuCo}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ during high-temperature pyrolysis. Figure 1a shows the formation process of CCP. When applied in LIBs as the anode, the composite delivered a $740\ \mathrm{mA}$ h $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ discharge capacity at $100~\mathrm{\mA}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ and good cycling reversibility (Figure 1d), along with a $99\%$ Coulombic efficiency. Similarly using Co-based MOF, Zhuang et  al. reported a facile bottom-up approach for the formation of highly dispersed supported $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ on N-doped porous carbon (N–C).[65] More specifically, ZIF-67 microcrystals were first grown on benzoic acid–modified covalent organic framework (COF), finally forming the ZIF-67/COF composite. What is noteworthy is that triggered by the high porosity and confining effect of COF, the product featured highly active crystal plane and large specific surface area. In addition, LIB tests showed a reversible $785~\mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at $500\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , along with a $99.65\%$ Coulombic efficiency. Otherwise, metallic tin $(\mathsf{S}\mathrm{n})$ has attracted tremendous attention as a promising anode material due to its large theoretical specific capacity of $993\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{~h~g^{-1}~}$ . Thereby, Dai et  al.[66] reported a $\mathrm{Sn/C}$ composite derived from the Sn-MOF precursor, in which ultrasmall Sn nanodots were evenly dispersed in N-doped mesoporous carbon matrix (denoted as $\mathrm{Sn}@\mathrm{N-C})$ . Benefiting from the synergetic combination between N–C and the ultrasmall Sn nanodots, the large volume change can be well accommodated and the structure integrity was preserved, thus contributing to an extremely long cycling stability with $507\mathrm{\mA}$ h $\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ remained after 1500 cycles. Furthermore, a well-designed porous pure carbon material with multifractal structures was recently reported by Li et al[113] via varying the conditions of the precursor pyrolysis process. This material was prepared by the vacuum carbonization of a zinc-based MOF, and demonstrated an ultrahigh lithium storage capacity of $2458~\mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , a favorable high rate performance and a desirable high rate performance, thus making fractal structures and the closed pore storage mode attract the public’s attention.
## 2.1.2. Carbon Nanosheets
Graphene has been widely studied and explored because of its unique structural and physicochemical properties of stable structure and long cycling life.[114–118] As a result, Tan et  al.[67] reported a N-doped graphene $/\mathrm{Fe}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{C}$ $(\mathrm{NG}/\mathrm{Fe}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{C})$ nanocomposite in 2014, which was prepared by a facile one-pot method using MIL-100 (Fe) (as shown in Figure 2b). The obtained material exhibited excellent electrochemical performances including a super high Coulombic efficiency of almost $100\%$ , superior rate capacity, discharge capacity of $1098\mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ after 48 cycles (Figure 2e), and remarkable cycling performance (as shown in Figure 2f). As depicted in Figure 2c, the $\mathrm{NG}/\mathrm{Fe}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{C}$ nanocomposite delivered a superior rate performance, the current density was $1000\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ while the discharge capacity was $609~\mathrm{{mA}}$ h $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ . As shown in Table 1, the Mn-containing materials always possess a higher capacitance than other electrode materials. Therefore, Chen et  al.[68] developed a distinguished two-step strategy (Figure 2a) for synthesizing MOF-derived $\mathrm{MnO}_{x}$ carbon sheet (CSs) nanocomposites. In this composite, the cube-like $\mathrm{MnO}_{x}$ nanoparticles were grown on the CSs and a solid electrolyte interface (SEI) layer was surrounded on the surface of the composite. Due to the SEI layer, $\mathrm{MnO}_{x}–\mathrm{CS}–600$ nanocomposite (the sample obtained at $600~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ performed a reversible capacity of $1217.70\mathrm{mAhg^{-1}}$ at $200\mathrm{mAg^{-1}}$ (Figure 2g) after 160 cycles. Notably, the discharge capacity remained as high as $612.10~\mathrm{mA}$ h $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ even at $2000\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (Figure 2d). Recently, ultrathin N-doped porous ultrathin carbon nanosheets (UT-CNSs) derived from a special $Z\mathrm{n}$ -based MOF were created through a low-cost bottom-up strategy to functionalize as working electrode for LIBs.[69] The UT-CNSs exhibited a considerable high $553~\mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at $10\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ and retained Coulombic efficiency of $100\%$ after a long cycle life of 1000 cycles at $2\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ . Furthermore, $\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ nanosheets and microporous nitrogendoped carbon dodecahedron (MNCD) composites were designed and manifested an outstanding reversible capacity of $915~\mathrm{mA}$ h $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ at $10\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ .[70] Moreover, Lin et  al.[119] produced a micro-nanostructured graphite/Si/reduced graphene oxide composite (SGG) recently. After 600 cycles, the obtained anode material delivered $467\mathrm{mA}$ h $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ at $700\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ .
## 2.1.3. Carbon Nanorod
Up to now, with the demerits of low theoretically charge ability $(372\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{\h\g}^{-1})$ ), traditional commercial graphite is no longer a major topic in LIB research and scientists are persistently researching for an alternative. Transition metal oxide anode materials generally present remarkable $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ -storage capacitance, but usually accompanied with large volume expansion and the inherent poor electrical conductivity during cycling, therefore resulting in limited rate performances. Since the nanorod structure may possess a larger specific surface area than the nanosheet, which is beneficial to the insertion/extraction process, enormous efforts have been devoted to prepare carbon nanorods. Early in 2015, Zheng et al.[71] reported a facile direct carbonization of MOF strategy for the in situ preparation of a porous carbon matrix, in which MnO nanocrystals were encapsulated (as shown in Figure 3b). The SEM image of the obtained $\mathsf{M n O@C}$ composites is shown in Figure 3c. The MnO nanocrystals were distributed uniformly into porous carbon matrix and the rod-like morphology was clearly observed in the image. In advantages of the produce of gaseous molecules and the high porosity of MOF precursor, which is beneficial to the pores formation and electron transfer, the as-prepared product performed a superior reversible capacity of $1221\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ after 100 cycles at $100\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (Figure 3e), together with an excellent rate capacity (Figure 3h). Furthermore, Sun et  al.[14] designed a Mn (terephthalic acid) (PTA)-MOF in which benzonic acid was implemented as a modulator to adjust the MOF assembly directions. As a result, the $\mathsf{M n O/C}$ spindle-like microrods (as shown in Figure 3d) obtained under certain condition manifested $1165~\mathrm{{mA}}$ h $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ at $300~\mathrm{{mA}}$ $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ (Figure 3j). Simultaneously, as Figure $3\mathrm{g}$ shows, the Coulombic efficiency at the first cycle was merely $56\%$ but quickly increased to above $98\%$ and kept stable in the subsequent cycles, which indicated a superior cycling stability. The $\mathrm{MnO-B_{4}/C}$ also behaved an excellent rate capability. Otherwise, Zhang et al.[72] built a $\mathsf{Z n O@C}$ core–shell nanorod array (NRA) on flexible carbon cloth to accommodate the volume change. Figure 3a shows the fabrication of $\mathrm{ZnO@C}$ core–shell nanostructure. Figure 3f manifests the cycling performance and Coulombic efficiency at $500\ \mathrm{\mA\\g^{-1}}$ . After 100 cycles, the anode retained $89\%$ of the first discharge capacity. The designed electrode also displayed a remarkable rate capability with steady discharge capacities of 1055, 913, 762, 591, and $530\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{\h\g}^{-1}$ at 100, 200, 400, 800, and $1000\ \mathrm{\mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively (as shown in Figure 3i).
## 2.1.4. Carbon Nanocage
Early in 2016, Liu et  al.[73] synthesized an original electrode material consisting of hollow cobalt sulfide nanoparticles inlayed in graphitic carbon nanocages $({\mathrm{HCSP}}{\mathsf{C G C C}})$ ) utilizing Co-based ZIF (ZIF-67) as the template under room temperature. The fabrication process and the SEM image of the uniform HCSP⊂GCC cathode were illustrated in Figure $^{4\mathrm{d},\mathrm{f},}$ respectively. The as-prepared material exhibited superior Li-ion storage capacities of 536, 489, 438, 393, 345, and $278~\mathrm{mA}$ h $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ at 0.20, 0.50, 1, 2, 5, and $10\mathrm{~C~}$ ( $1\mathrm{~C~}=544\mathrm{~mA~g^{-1})}$ , respectively, suggesting a superior rate capability and stable cycling performance (Figure 4e). Even at a high current density of $1\mathrm{{C}}$ , the cathode can still deliver a reversible and stable average capacity as high as $366\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{~h~g^{-1}~}$ after 150 cycles. Similarly utilizing Co-based MOF, Zhou et  al.[74] designed a composite material of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Co}\ @$ carbon nanocages (COCCNCs). It was synthesized through two steps of annealing of ZIF-67. The asprepared COCCNCs was used to test electrochemical performance, and a discharge capacity of $1158\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and a charge capacity of $867\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ can easily be reached in the first cycle, followed by $75\%$ Coulombic efficiency. When the current density was $100\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , $850~\mathrm{mA}$ h $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ reversible specific capacity was achieved. Additionally, a superior rate capability and an excellent cycling stability were accomplished both in good manners. Not long after this, a novel hollow porous $\mathrm{{}Z n O/C}$ nanocage was synthesized via a one-step pyrolysis of hollow MOF material (MOF-5) at $500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ as both precursor and template, and it presented a surprisingly high specific surface areas of $256\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (Figure 4a).[75] Figure 4c indicates the morphology of the hollow porous $\mathrm{{ZnO/C}}$ nanocage. The resultant sample released an initial discharge capacity of $1928~\mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at the current density of $100\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , and remained $750~\mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ after 100 cycles. The charge/discharge capacities for the first cycle of $\mathsf{Z n O@C}$ were 1808 and $942~\mathrm{mA}$ h $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ , respectively. Notably, according to Figure 4b, the $\mathrm{{ZnO/C}}$ even obtained a rate capacity of $351~\mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at a relatively high $2000\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . After a long time research, Huang et  al. tried to combine two different MOFs to see what would happen, so they annealed a ZIF-8 $\textmu m$ ZIF-67 precursor at $800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under Ar protection. In the end, they gained a porous bimetallic $\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{Zn}$ embedded N-doped carbon polyhedral nanocage (as shown in Figure 4i).[76] Figure ${4\mathrm{g}}$ shows the illustration of synthesizing $\mathrm{Co{-}Z n/N{-}C}$ nanocage. When evaluated as anode material of LIBs, the product exhibited initial 863.30 and $509.60~\mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ discharge and charge capacities, respectively. Moreover, as can be seen in Figure 4h, the rate capacity of $444{\mathrm{~mA~h~g^{-1}}}$ can be obtained even at $2000\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ .
## 2.1.5. Others
In the early years, iron oxides were proved to be alternative candidates for $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ storage because of their natural abundance, high theoretical reversible capacities, and environmental friendliness. For example, Li et  al.[120] synthesized a porous carbon-decorated iron oxides through annealing iron MOF precursors. The as-prepared ${\mathrm{C-Fe}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ particles presented microcuboid-like morphologies and exhibited wonderful electrochemical performance. Shortly after, $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{C}$ nanocomposites possessing octahedral structures were prepared by Chen et al.[121] for LIBs. Taking advantages of the high electrical conductivity of the carbon matrix and the porous structure of the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{C}$ nanocomposites, these $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{C}$ octahedral nanostructures also demonstrated a great lithium storage capacity.
In another approach, in 2016, a $\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.19}\mathrm{S}@\mathrm{N}$ , S-codoped carbon dodecahedron with rooted carbon nanotubes (Co– $\mathrm{Zn-S}@\mathrm{NS-C-CNT})$ was successfully prepared by Li et  al.[77] from a bimetal–organic frameworks (Co/Zn-ZIF-67). Benefiting from the synergistic effect of two metal sulfide species, N, S-codoped carbon overlayer, mesoporous dodecahedron structure, and deep-rooted CNTs network, the composite exhibited $941\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{~h~g^{-1}~}$ after 250 cycles. After 500 cycles, large reversible capacities of 734, 591, and $505\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{~h~g^{-1}~}$ were still remained at 1, 2, and $5{\mathrm{~A~g}}^{-1}$ , respectively. Later, a brand solvent-free approach was designed to construct uniform MOF shell-derived carbon confined $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Co}$ nanocubes $(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/$ $\operatorname{Co}(\varnothing\operatorname{C})$ .[78] The fabrication process is shown in Figure 5f. This was the first report of an MOF-coated metal oxide acquired by a simple and scalable thermal solid-phase reaction. Figure 5e shows the SEM image of the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Co}@\mathrm{C}$ nanocubes. The rate capability was tested by alternately discharging and charging at various current densities (Figure 5g). As we can see in the figure, extraordinary high reversible specific capacities of about 1030, 950, 850, 740, 650, 520, and $350~\mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ were delivered at 0.10, 0.20, 0.50, 1, 2, 5, and $10\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ , respectively. Moreover, the capacity quickly returned to $980~\mathrm{{mA}}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ when the current density was finally reduced to $100\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , demonstrating superior stability of the anode. Recently, a foam-like $\operatorname{CoO}(\varnothing\mathrm{N}$ , S-codoped carbon hybrid composite was synthesized and characterized via thermal treatment.[60] The binary doping of heteroatoms into carbon nanomaterials does boost the electrochemical activities of these nanomaterials, resulting in a stable reversible capacity, and the specific discharge and charge capacities were maintained at stable values of about 812 and $809\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively, at $1\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ over 500 cycles.
Recently, an ultrafine $Z\mathrm{nS}$ nanorod rooted in the hollow carbon polyhedra (denoted as $\mathrm{ZnS~NR}@\mathrm{HCP})$ was synthesized via an in situ formation strategy (Figure 5a).[79] The hollow hybrid composites obtained at $600~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ are shown in Figure 5b, and a highly porous and robust structure was achieved. The as-prepared material manifested excellent lithium-storage performance. For example, average specific capacities of 1388, 1063, 962, 824, and $608\ \mathrm{mA}\mathrm{h}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ were delivered at the current densities of 100, 200, 400, 800, and $1600\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively, and the capacity could maintain at $1108~\mathrm{{mA}}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ when the current density reached $200\mathrm{\mA\g^{-1}}$ (Figure 5d), thus illustrating an excellent reversible capability. Not long before this hollow structure, NiSb⊂CHSs (NiSb-embedded carbon hollow spheres) were designed by Yu et al.[80] utilizing hollow Ni-MOFs with ${\mathrm{SbCl}}_{3}$ via facile annealing and galvanic replacement reactions (Figure 5c). The NiSb⊂CHSs preserved the advantages of ideal porosity of Ni-MOFs (as shown in Figure 5h). Notably, this material was used in Li-ion storage for the first time and demonstrated a good electrochemical performance. When combined with a nanolevel $\mathrm{LiMn}_{2}{\sf O}_{4}$ cathode, the $\mathrm{NiSbeCHSs//LiMn_{2}O_{4}}$ full cell characterized a high rate capability ( $210\ \mathrm{\mA}$ h $\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $2000\ \mathrm{\mA\g^{-1})}$ and $99\%$ Coulombic efficiency. In addition, carbon-coated $\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{5}$ nanoparticles were synthesized via a facile synthetic process using as-synthesized MIL-47(V) as both vanadium and carbon precursors with controlled heat treatment. Among many other materials with different morphologies, few of them can display the electrochemical performance as good as the materials mentioned above.[81]
## 2.2. LSBs
LSBs are attracting remarkable attention as promising nextgeneration batteries due to their high theoretical capacity $(1675\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ ), high energy density $(2500~\mathrm{W}\mathrm{{h}\ k g^{-1})}$ , and the low cost of sulfur.[122,123] A major problem hampering LSBs commercialization is their rapid fading of capacity, which is limited by the complicated reduction/oxidation pathway of the Li-S chemistry.[124–127] Herein, hierarchically porous carbon nanoplates (HPCN) were reported to act as cathode material for LSBs.[82] The resultant material exhibited $730\mathrm{mAhg^{-1}}$ after 50 cycles at $0.50\mathrm{C}$ , and the Coulombic efficiency was up to $97\%$ (as shown in Figure 6b), showing an excellent cycling stability.
Interestingly, the HPCN was prepared through a simple and rational way, by direct carbonization of MOF-5, and that is quite different from those complicated synthetic processes reported before.[128–131] Coincidentally, on the same date, Xi et  al.[83] presented a similar material prepared in a same manner. Differently, this report mainly described the impact of the pore volume and size distribution of the carbonized sulfur encapsulating MOFs on the capacity and the cycle life of LSB. Xi et al. prepared four materials by pyrolysis of different MOFs. It is proved that cell cathodes containing porous carbon from carbonizing of ZIF-8 and solvo-MOF-5 (solvothermally synthesized MOF-5) showed remarkable improvement in cycling behavior as they maintained a high content of micropores ( $16.15\%$ for carbonized ZIF-8 and $62\%$ for carbonized solvo-MOF-5). Micropores help improve cycle life by effectively confining polysulfide anion diffusion in the organic electrolyte as long as the proportion of macropores is negligible and macropores are responsible for the capacity loss, which can be proved by carbonized ZIF-8 and slvo-MOF-5 too. Batteries based on carbonized solvo-MOF-5 with $10.32\%$ of macropores showed greater loss in capacity than carbonized ZIF-8 with negligible macropores. Moreover, Li et al.[132] reported a similar material.
Different from HPCN from Xu et al., the MOF-derived carbon (MOF-C) was fabricated through an in situ ammonia treatment, and therefore obtained a sturdy tunable structure, which can be observed in Figure 6d. In advantage of $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ treatment, the carbon material with 7 min ${\mathrm{NH}}_{3}$ treatment (N7-C) possessed a pore volume rose of $1.30\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , which is twice of the ZIF8-C, resulting in a better capacity retention than others. Later in the same year, microporous carbon polyhedrons (MPCPs) were prepared from ZIF-8 by Wu et  al.[84] The as-prepared product exhibited abundant and uniform micropores as well as a stable cycling performance. The structure feature and cycling stability of the MPCP-S composites are shown in Figure 6e,c. The capacity quickly decayed at first and steadied at around $250\mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ after 100 cycles in DOL/DME electrolyte based on the mass of sulfur with $63\mathrm{\wt\%}$ of sulfur content. Although the capacity decay is less severe in the TEG/DME electrolyte, the cycling stability is lower than expected. In another work, a hierarchical graphene sheet-sulfur/carbon (GSS/C) composite was prepared by an ultrasonic method.[85] The composite exhibited an enhanced electrochemical performance when compared with $\mathsf{S}/\mathsf{C}$ composite. Moreover, nitrogen-doped carbon spheres with abundant meso- and micropores were obtained by direct carbonization of nitrogen-containing MOF nanocrystals.[86] Figure 6a shows the fabrication process. In the merits of N-doping and N–S interactions, the NDC–S hybrid cathode exhibited a superior electrochemical performance. The $\mathsf{C}{-}\mathsf{S}$ hybrid obtained after pyrolysis at $300^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ (noted as C–S-3) showed a brilliant $1008.70~\mathrm{{mA}}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ after 50 cycles, and a capacity retention of $82.60\%$ was achieved even after 100 cycles. Besides, a good rate capability is investigated in Figure 6f, the specific capacity can retain at $632~\mathrm{{mA}~h~g^{-1}}$ at $5\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ . In addition, a novel 1D french fries–like hierarchical porous carbon (FLHPC) was built by carbonizing an aluminum-MOF (Al-MOF).[87] Not only did the final composite deliver a high initial discharge capacity of nearly $1200~\mathrm{{mA}}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at $167.20~\mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , but showed a good capacity retention of $68\%$ at $836~\mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ after 200 cycles.
Furthermore, a reduced graphene oxide (RGO) covered cobalt-doped porous carbon polyhedrons was utilized for sulfur immobilizers (denoted as $\mathsf{R G O/C-C o-S})$ as cathode for high-performance LSB.[88] The final product was synthesized through a carbonization process and exhibited greatly enhanced electrochemical performance. As illustrated in Figure $7\mathrm{g}$ , a perfect cycling stability was fulfilled with a capacity of $949\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{~h~g^{-1}~}$ at 300th cycle. The Coulombic efficiency maintained more than $99\%$ throughout the whole cycling process. Meanwhile, specific capacities of 772, 704, and $606~\mathrm{{mA}}$ h $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ were delivered at 0.50, 1, and $2\mathrm{A}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively (as shown in Figure 7c), which stood for a great rate capability. Shortly after, a self-template and organic solvent-free approach was proposed by Jiang et  al.[89] to establish N, S-codoped carbon nanosheets derived from monoclinic ZIF-8 (denoted as ZIF-8-NS-C) under room temperature. The superior rate capability of sulfur/ZIF8-NS-C cathode was demonstrated to behave high capacities of $1226\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $0.20\mathrm{C}$ and $785~\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{~h~g^{-1}}$ at $2\mathrm{~C~}$ . The cycling performance is revealed in Figure 7d, and the cathode delivered $887\mathrm{mAhg^{-1}}$ discharge capacity and $99\%$ Coulombic efficiency. Meanwhile, a low decay rate of $0.12\%$ in every single cycle was realized within 300 cycles. Simultaneously, a new bifunctional 3D nitrogen-rich carbon photonic crystal architecture with an extremely large surface area $(2546\ m^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})$ was designed by combining silica templating with in situ texturing of MOFs.[90] The excellent rate performance is shown in Figure 7e, even when the rate increasing to $2\mathrm{~C~}$ , the bicontinuous hierarchical porous carbon obtained at $950~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ coated with sulfur (labeled as $\operatorname{BHPC-950@S}$ ) battery still delivers capacities as much as $967\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . Figure $76$ demonstrates a superb cycling stability when hosting $78\mathrm{wt\%}$ sulfur, the decay rate over 1000 cycles can be $0.05\%$ per cycle at $0.50\mathrm{C}$ . Furthermore, three flower-shaped microporous nitrogen-doped carbon nanosheets were obtained from temperature-controlled carbonization of the $Z\mathrm{n}$ -based MOF (namely FMNCN-800, FMNCN-900, and FMNCN-1000), and the corresponding schematic for preparation is given in Figure 7a.[91] After experimental verification, the resulting $\mathbf{S}@$ FMNCN-900 composite presented excellent electrochemical properties in terms of superb reversible capacities and stable cycling performance. After cycling for 200 cycles, the capacity maintained $1220\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{\h\g}^{-1}$ at $0.10\mathrm{~C~}$ , and after an ultralong 1000 cycles, it could remain $727\mathrm{mA}$ h $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ at $2\mathrm{C}$ with a capacity loss of only $0.02\%$ every cycle. The average discharge capacities are displayed in Figure 7f, with 1261, 1200, 1117, 900, and $759\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{\h\g}^{-1}$ at 0.10, 0.20, 0.50, 2, and $5\mathrm{~C~}$ , respectively, and restored to $1254\mathrm{mA}$ h $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ as the C-rate switched from $5\mathrm{C}$ back to $0.10\mathrm{C}$ . Nevertheless, there are also many other Li–S batteries with a carbon material derived from different kinds of MOFs as their electrodes, and all of them performed ideal electrochemical performances.[83,133,134]
## 2.3. SIBs
Because of the natural abundance, costless, and electrochemical activities of sodium-ion-related materials, SIBs are acknowledged as the most promising alternatives to LIBs.[135] Nevertheless, the practical implementation of SIBs is obstructed by poor cycling stability and low energy density. The poor cycle life is accused of the larger ionic radius of $\mathrm{\DeltaNa^{+}}$ $55\%$ larger than $\lvert\mathrm{Li^{+}}\rvert$ , which makes it more difficult to reversibly insert and extract.[136] Therefore, the exploitation of suitable electrode materials is crucial for further development of SIBs.
## 2.3.1. Metal Oxide/Carbon Composites
As we all know, transition metal oxides $(\mathrm{M}_{x}\mathrm{O}_{\gamma},$ $\l{\textbf{M}}=\l{\bf M}{\bf n}$ , Fe, Ni, Co, Sn, etc.) always possess the higher theoretical capacities (around $1000\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{\h\g^{-1}})^{[137]}$ compared to graphitic materials, and as a consequence, they have attracted much attention in SIBs anode material. Meanwhile, it must be a great enhancement when combining the advantages of transition metal oxides and carbon materials as they might possess large surface area and high $\mathrm{{Na^{+}}}$ storage capacity at the same time. Therefore, Zou et  al.[92] designed hollow ball-in-ball nanostructure, in which NiO/Ni nanocrystal was covered by a graphene shell (as shown in Figure 8a,b). The well-designed product provided an average capacities of 385, 295, 248, and 207 mA h $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ when cycled at 200, 500, 1000, and $2000\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively (Figure 8d). Figure 8c exhibits a fading rate of only $0.20\%$ per cycle, declaring a good cycle stability. Otherwise, Wang et al.[93] reported a $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{N}$ -doped C (NC) derived from ZIF-67 synthesized by a controllable two-step annealing procedure. Benefiting from the nitrogen-doped carbon coating, the electronic conductivity of the product, the volume changes of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ were greatly improved. The low magnification SEM image is shown in Figure 8e. Excellent rate capability is displayed in Figure 8g from the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{NC}$ electrode, which showed average capacities of 317, 276, and $263~\mathrm{\mA}$ h $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ at 400, 800, and $1000\ \mathrm{\mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. A capacity loss rate of $0.03\%$ per cycle was found over 1100 cycles at $1000\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (as shown in Figure 8f). Recently, similarly using Co-based MOF, a Ni-doped ${\mathrm{Co}}/{\mathrm{CoO}}/{\mathrm{N-C}}$ hybrid was fabricated and also performed wonderful specific capacity and good cycling stability.[94] More complicatedly, a bimetallic Ni-Co-ZIF was used as the starting precursor. Furthermore, hierarchical hollow hybrid $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}101/\mathrm{C}$ materials were prepared as anode materials of SIBs by employing a generic template-free strategy.[95] The hollow nanostructure ensured a stable reversible capacity of $710\ \mathrm{mA}\mathrm{h}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , and after 200 cycles, it can retain at $662~\mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ with a retention of $93.20\%$ . This characteristic can also be confirmed by the rate performance. Shortly after, Zou et  al. designed a total new electrode material. After in situ pyrolysis of titanium-based MOF crystals, a carbon-coated rutile titanium dioxide (CRT) was constructed and then tested as a SIBs anode material.[96] The CRT showed a better electronic conductivity and larger specific surface area $(245~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ when compared with pure titanium dioxide $(12.80\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1})$ . In addition, the CRT electrode exhibited $\approx175\ensuremath{\mathrm{~mA~h~g^{-1}~}}$ at $0.50~\mathrm{C}$ -rate after 200 cycles and $70\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{h}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ after 2000 cycles at a high C-rate of 20, which represented an excellent rate capability. Hereafter, $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ composites derived from graphene oxide wrapped MOFs $(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{-MIL}\mathrm{-}125\$ $(\mathrm{Ti})$ ) with porous structure[97] were produced, and delivered an excellent capacity retention of $93\%$ even after 5000 cycles. Otherwise, $\mathsf{M n O}@\mathsf{C}$ nanorods,[138] and $\mathsf{C u O}_{I}$ reduced graphene oxide composites[139] were both prepared but did not perform well in SIBs anode material tests.
## 2.3.2. Metal Sulfide/Carbon Composites
Recently, transition metal sulfides have shown promising applications in SIBs. For instance, a $\mathrm{Ni_{3}S_{2}/C o_{9}S_{8}/N-C}$ composite with a hollow-sphere structure was prepared after carbonization and sulfurization of the binary Ni-Co-MOF via a facile solvothermal reaction.[98] The SEM image of the composite is shown in Figure 9j. Benefiting from the ultrafine metal sulfide nanoparticle, hollow porous structure, and ultrathin N-doped carbon, the final composite presented exceptional electrochemical performances containing a $419.90\mathrm{mAhg^{-1}}$ after 100 cycles at 0.10 A $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ , an outstanding capacity fading rate of merely $0.01\%$ per cycle (Figure 9e), and a superior rate capability (Figure 9f). Furthermore, taking advantage of ZIF-8 derived structure, ${\mathrm{ZnS-Sb}}_{2}{\mathrm{S}}_{3}@{\mathrm{C}}$ core-double shell polyhedron structure was synthesized through a sulfurization reaction between $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ dissociated from ZIF-8 and $\mathsf{S}^{2-}$ from thioacetamide.[99] The polyhedral composite showed a considerably improved electrochemical performance with a stable cycling stability, high Coulombic efficiency and enhanced specific capacity. Ren et al.[100] reported the design of ultrathin $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ nanosheets coated with MOF-derived N–C nanowall array hybrids on flexible carbon cloth $(\mathbf{CC}@\mathbf{CN}@\mathbf{MoS}_{2})$ as a free-standing anode for high-performance SIBs. The as-fabricated $\mathbf{CC}@\mathbf{CN}@\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ electrode exhibited a superior electrochemical property when evaluated as an anode for SIB. It performed $619.20~\mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at $200\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ after 100 cycles, an ultralong cycling life stability with $265\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{\g}^{-1}$ at $1000\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ after 1000 cycles and excellent rate capability. The excellent electrochemical performance could be attributed to the unique 2D hybrid structures, which shorten the ion diffusion paths and enlarge the $\mathrm{{Na^{+}}}$ insertion/ extraction spaces. Meanwhile, the porous N-doped carbon nanowall arrays improved conductivity of $\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ and maintained structural integrity of MOF.
## 2.3.3. Others
Carbonaceous materials have attracted tremendous attention as anodes for SIBs, For example, Shi et  al.[101] successfully synthesized a nitrogen-doped porous carbon (N–C) early in 2015 with ZIF-8 as the precursor at a high carbonization temperature of $950~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . When testing for SIBs anode, the asprepared N–C material exhibited a favorable rate capability and cycling stability. Zou et  al.[102] obtained a cube-shaped porous carbon (CPC) from direct carbonization of MOF-5 crystals and showed a large surface area and good electrical conductivity, as well as a high overall sodium storage capacity and high specific capacity. Besides, an innovative sulfur doping method was designed with MOF-5 as templated and carbon precursor, and inorganic sulfur powders as sulfur sources.[103] Benefiting from the introduction of S atoms into carbon structure, the obtained sulfur-doped mesoporous carbon (SPC) held a longterm cycling stability and an outstanding rate capacity. Due to the similar chemical nature of phosphorus and sulfur, red phosphorus (P) has attracted intense attention as promising anode material for high energy density SIBs. Ren et al.[58] demonstrated a rational strategy by modifying nanosized amorphous red P into a nitrogen-doped microporous carbon matrix (noted as $\mathrm{P}@\mathrm{N-MPC}$ , as shown in Figure 9i) to improve the sodium storage performance of red P. The fabrication process is illustrated in Figure 9a. As shown in Figure 9d, the $\mathrm{P}@\mathrm{N}.$ - MPC displayed reversible capacities of 684, 570, 494, 470, 414, 365, 315, and $291~\mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at 0.30, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, and 9 A $\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. In addition, the $\operatorname{P}({\widehat{\varrho}}\operatorname{N-MPC}$ also performed an improved rate capacity of about $450~\mathrm{mA}$ h $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ at
$1\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ after 1000 cycles with a surprisingly low capacity loss of $0.02\%$ per cycle (Figure ${\mathfrak{g}}_{\mathfrak{g}}^{\cdot}$ ). Therefore, as a result of the good performance of red P in electrochemical tests, a novel strategy was developed to synthesize CoP $@{\mathsf{C}}$ -RGO-NF as a binderfree anode for high-performance SIBs through an in situ low-temperature phosphidation process from ZIF-67 derived core/shell $\mathrm{Co}@\mathrm{C}$ polyhedral structures. Inside of the ${\mathrm{CoP@C}}.$ RGO-NF, there are MOF derived core/shell CoP $@{\mathrm C}$ polyhedrons anchoring on 3D reduced graphene oxide on nickel foam (NF).[104] The SEM image of the unique CoP $@$ C-RGO-NF is shown in Figure 9h. The electrode exhibited remarkable electrochemical performances involving outstanding cycling stability and high rate capacity. The galvanostatic discharge– charge profiles for the initial five cycles of the Co $\mathsf{P}@\mathsf{C}$ -RGO-NF electrode are shown in Figure 9c, and reversible capacities at different current densities are shown in Figure 9b. Significantly, a capacity of $155~\mathrm{mA}$ h $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ can be maintained even at $1600\ \mathrm{\mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . More importantly, the capacity can come back to $540.30\mathrm{\mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ when the current density decreases to $100\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , showing a superior reversible stability.
## 2.4. Others
In addition to LIBs and SIBs, there are other types of batteries exhibiting ideal electrochemical performance. Some instances are listed as follows.
Researchers have paid tremendous attention in $\mathrm{Li}{-}\mathsf{O}_{2}$ batteries because of their high energy density. For example, porous carbon $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ composites obtained via a post-thermal treatment showing an improved electrochemical performance as the cathode in rechargeable $\mathrm{Li}{-}\mathsf{O}_{2}$ batteries.[105] In advantage of the mesoporous structure, a low overpotential and high reversible capacity $(9850\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{\h\g}^{-1};$ were achieved at $100\mathrm{\mA\g^{-1}}$ , along with an excellent cycling stability after 320 cycles. Not long before that work, FeCo alloy core $@\mathrm{N-C}$ shell nanoparticles anchored on CNTs were synthesized through effective pyrolysis of a bimetal–organic framework (Fe/Co MOF) with CNTs.[106] A first discharge/charge specific capacity of more than $5000~\mathrm{{mA}}$ h ${\mathrm{g}_{\mathrm{cat}}}^{-1}$ at a constant current density of $100\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{g_{cat}}^{-1}$ was found in the $\mathrm{Li}{-}\mathsf{O}_{2}$ battery. Apart from $\mathrm{Li}{-}\mathsf{O}_{2}$ batteries, Li–Se batteries are the alternative to $\mathrm{Li}{-}\mathsf{O}_{2}$ batteries, as selenium (Se) has a similar (de)lithiation mechanism to that of sulfur. Li et al.[107] developed nitrogen-doped carbon sponges (NCS) composed of hierarchical microporous carbon layers via directly carbonizing from MOFs under Ar and ${\mathrm{NH}}_{3}$ flow. Se was decorated into the micropores by melting-diffusion and infiltration methods. When serving as a cathode sample for a Li–Se battery, the NCS/Se-50 composite, which contains $50\mathrm{wt\%}$ Se, exhibited a capacity of $443.20\mathrm{mAhg^{-1}}$ at the $200\mathrm{{th}}$ cycle and a Coulombic efficiency of $99.90\%$ at $337.50\mathrm{mAhg^{-1}}$ , which was related to a $0.03\%$ capacity decrease in every single cycle from the 5th to 200th. Moreover, Liu et  al.[108] delivered a novel structure in which selenium nanoparticles were uniformly dispersed in the hierarchical porous structure of carbon microcubes (CMCs), which were produced from a typical meltdiffusion process. With the same Se loading of $50\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ , the yielded Se/CMCs composite exhibited a brilliant discharge capacity of $780.40\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{\h\g}^{-1}$ at $0.20\mathrm{~C~}$ and $425.20~\mathrm{mA}$ h $\dot{\mathrm{\bf~g}}^{-1}$ after 100 cycles. In addition, a 3D structural cobalt and nitrogen-codoped graphitic carbon (labeled as $\scriptstyle\mathrm{C-Co-N},$ ), was established by He et  al.[109] With a Se content of $76.50\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ , the C–Co–N/Se cathode demonstrated an excellent reversible capacity $672.30\mathrm{\mA}$ h $\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , $99.60\%$ of the theoretical value) and good cycling stability (only $0.07\%$ fading per cycle and nearly $100\%$ Coulombic efficiency). Numerous efforts have been devoted in solar cell, potassium-ion and other batteries to improve performance with MOF derived carbon materials to be their electrodes as well.[140–144]
## 2.5. Summary
After reviewing the above descriptions and summarizing from Table 1, we can safely draw the conclusion that a considerable number of MOF-derived carbon materials have been applied to batteries by controlling the carbonization temperature, adjusting various morphologies and varying reaction conditions. Among these efforts, the Ti-MOF deriving carbon materials have the best recycling performance, which can maintain a good electrical capacity after thousands of cycles, followed by the Cocontaining materials. In spite of cyclic performance, the carbon materials derived from Mn-MOF have the highest capacitance. Taking the cost of materials, reaction conditions and the impact on the environment into account, Co-MOFs are proven to behave better as precursors. For LIBs application, $\operatorname{CoO}(\varnothing\mathrm{N}$ , S-C behaved better in electrochemical testing. CoO is well known for its high lithium storage capacity, and meanwhile, carbon-based materials compensate for their poor conductivity. Furthermore, N, S-codoping would change the charge redistribution, increase the electron delocalization and promote the creation of charged sites $\left(\mathrm{C^{+}}\right)$ , as nitrogen atoms are an electron donor and sulfur atoms have larger electronegativity. As for Li–S batteries, the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{RGO}}/{\mathrm{C}}-{\mathrm{Co}}-{\mathrm{S}}$ have successfully achieved a much higher reversible capacity when compared with other samples. The presence of abundant pores, the uniform insertion of ultrafine Co nanoparticles and the tightly wrapped RGO nanosheets are beneficial for the immobilization of S species. To take a further step, the volume changes in the charge–discharge process can be ameliorated by a porous carbon matrix with Co particles and RGO nanosheets. When considering SIBs, the P-based material, confined with nitrogen-doped carbon, as an SIB electrode material shows better electrochemical performance than other composites. When utilized as anode for SIBs, P can deliver a high theoretical sodium-storage capacity of $2595~\mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ because the electrochemical reaction between sodium and P, the porous matrix structure and the synergy effect in the $\mathrm{P}@\mathrm{NMPC}$ composite can greatly enhance the sodium storage performance.
## 3. Supercapacitors
Electrochemical capacitors, also acknowledged as supercapacitors, are electrochemical energy storage devices that provide higher power density than batteries and higher energy density than conventional dielectric capacitors.[145–147] SCs possess great advantages of rapid charge and discharge and highly prolonged cycle life. However, they may suffer from low energy density compared to batteries. Supercapacitors may be much more complicated devices when using different types of electrodes, since composite electrodes can perform both capacitive and faradaic charge storage. Here, we will summarize some typical SCs classified by their electrode materials. Some representative electrode materials and their corresponding capacity characteristics are listed in Table 2.
## 3.1. Nanoporous Carbon
In view of the superior electrochemical properties of NPC derived from MOF, researchers have experimentally applied them to supercapacitors. $Z\mathrm{n}$ -based MOFs are some of the most commonly used MOF materials in recent years and exhibit desirable performance. Early in 2009, NPC was prepared by poly­ merizing and then carbonizing a carbon precursor (MOF-5).[148] When tested as an electrode material for supercapacitors, all asprepared samples displayed ideal capacitor performance. The product obtained at $600~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ showed almost constant specific capacitance above $100\mathrm{~F~g^{-1}}$ at $5~\mathrm{mV}~\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ , while the NPC sample prepared at $530~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ possessed the highest BET surface area of $3040\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (Figure 10b,e). With the deep research of carbon materials, a variety of NPC materials have been designed, optimized performance was explored, and these materials have been applied into commercial practice. The lithium-ion hybrid supercapacitors (Li-HSC), which contain a Li-ion battery-type anode and an electrochemical double layer capacitance (EDLC)- type cathode, have drawn much attention because of the large energy density. Banerjee et al.[149] demonstrated a Li-HSC with 3D carbon cuboids as the cathode material and $\mathrm{Li}_{4}\mathrm{Ti}_{5}\mathrm{O}_{12}$ as the anode material. One of the advantages of $\mathrm{Li}_{4}\mathrm{Ti}_{5}\mathrm{O}_{12}$ is that they do not form SEI layers, thus efficiently reduced the initial capacity loss. This novel design performed a maximum specific capacity of $149\mathrm{~F~g^{-1}}$ , a high energy density $(65~\mathrm{W~h~kg^{-1}})$ and have $82\%$ capacitance remained after 10 000 galvanostatic cycles. Likewise, $\mathrm{\DeltaXu}$ et al.[150] designed a Li-HSC with a unique hierarchical carbon-coated $\mathrm{WO}_{3}$ $\mathrm{(WO}_{3}/\mathrm{C})$ mesoporous structure as the anode and MOF-derived $_{\mathrm{N-C}}$ hollow polyhedra as the cathode. Li-HSC exhibited a high energy density of
160 W $\mathrm{{h}\ k g^{-1}}$ at a power density of 173.60 W $\mathrm{kg^{-1}}$ , and retained a capacity period of $88.30\%$ at $5\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ after 3000 chargingdischarging cycles.
Moreover, an original symmetric supercapacitors based on NPC material was designed by Salunkhe et  al.[17] via directly carbonizing from $Z\mathrm{n}$ -based MOFs without using any other precursors. The schematic presentation of preparation procedure is shown in Figure 10a. As demonstrated in Figure 10c, the asprepared materials exhibited a maximum specific capacitance of $251\mathrm{~F~g^{-1}}$ in 1 m $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ electrolyte at $5~\mathrm{mV~s^{-1}}$ , which was much higher than most of the NPC values measured in an aqueous electrolyte using a three electrode arrangement at that time. The symmetric SC studies also showed a high stability and good rate capability. However, it is still unable to meet the next generation energy storage demand. Yi et al.[151] developed a novel asymmetric supercapacitor utilizing carbon nanotube $@$ nickel oxide nanosheet (CNT $\vec{\ @}\mathrm{NiO})$ core–shell composites as anode material. In addition to ultralong cycling stability $(93\%$ capacitance retention after 10 000 cycles), a 25.40 W h $\ k g^{-1}$ maximum energy density was achieved when the power density was 400 W $\mathrm{kg^{-1}}$ . Remarkably, a “brick-and-mortar” sandwiched porous carbon was successfully built in which the porous carbon film derived from MOF-5 was the “mortar” and the graphene nanosheet was the “bricks.”[152] The fabrication process is depicted in Figure 10d. The product demonstrated outstanding cycling stability $(99\%$ capacitance retention after 10 000 cycles) and high capacitance of $345\mathrm{~F~g^{-1}}$ under $2~\mathrm{mV}~\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ (as shown in Figure 10i). Notably, the as-assembled symmetric supercapacitor displayed a high energy density of $30.30\mathrm{~W~h~kg}^{-1}$ in an aqueous electrolyte at a power density of $137~\mathrm{W~kg^{-1}}$ and had excellent cycling capacity with $94\%$ capacitance retention after $10\ 000$ cycles, which is much higher than previously reported in symmetric and asymmetric supercapacitors. Another NPC material was synthesized through controlled carbonization of bimetallic ${\mathrm{Co/Zn–ZIF}}$ at $800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and subsequent oxidation at $250{-}300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ [153] The resulting product is a composite containing N-doped nanoporous carbon with a carbon-rich nanotubes (CNT) on particle surfaces, carbon coexisting with Co nanoparticles (NPs) and $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ . The synthesis and functional application are shown in Figure $10\mathrm{g}$ . The optimized structure demonstrated a large surface area of $202~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , and the final MOF-derived nanoarchitecture displayed a superior capacity of $545\mathrm{~F~g^{-1}}$ , achieving $320\%$ enhanced capacitance compared to that of pristine nanoporous carbon, therefore reaching a new high level for hybrid supercapacitors (Figure 10f) and indicating a path to a new family of MOF-derived NPC materials. In other reports, nitrogen doping of carbon materials has been proven to improve performance in electrochemical testing previously in our investigation, and thus tremendous efforts were devoted to exploring their performance in supercapacitors. For example, a N-doped carbon anode was prepared through a “one for two” strategy by transforming a single 2D MOF precursor into a flexible asymmetric supercapacitor.[154] Otherwise, a nitrogen-doped MOF-based porous carbon fiber (NPCF) was synthesized through the pyrolysis of MOF nanofibers to optimize electrode performance.[25] Due to its unique 1D hollow structure, the N-doped NPCF exhibited a great energy storage capacity of $332\ensuremath{\mathrm{~F~}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ (Figure 10h). Another N-doped hierarchical porous carbon was prepared in a one-step synthetic route by introducing active site $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ into the pores of MOF precursors, and as a result, this novel structure enhanced both the electrochemical performance of these porous carbons and their $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture properties with $97\%$ capacity retention after cycling 10 000 times.[155]
## 3.2. Metal Oxide/Carbon Composites
When metal oxides were used for supercapacitors, they behaved a higher energy density than carbonaceous materials and a better electrochemical stability compared with polymer materials. Unfortunately, the unsatisfactory electronic conductivities and electrochemical windows have limited their widespread practical applications.[163] Therefore, researchers have attempted to combine the advantages of transition metal oxides and carbon-based materials. Orthogonal $\mathrm{Nb}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{5}$ quantum dots embedded in N–C referred to as NQD-NC, were prepared to fabricate hybrid supercapacitors (HSCs).[156] Luckily, the product showed superior electrochemical performance in terms of a maximum energy density of 76.90 W h $\mathrm{kg^{-1}}$ observed at the power density of $450~\mathrm{W~kg^{-1}}$ , as well as a maximum power density. A superior cyclic stability was achieved with a capacity retention of $85\%$ at $5{\mathrm{~A~g}}^{-1}$ after 4500 cycles in the range of $0.50{-}3\mathrm{~V}.$ Furthermore, asymmetric supercapacitors were developed using the $\mathsf{M O F-N P C/M n O_{2}}$ hybrids as positive electrodes.[157] The fabrication process is shown in Figure 11a. After pseudocapacitive materials were incorporated into the porous structures, the performance of as-prepared $\mathsf{M O F-N P C/M n O_{2}}$ was greatly improved, exhibiting the maximum energy density of 76 W h $\mathrm{kg^{-1}}$ at the power density of $2.20\ensuremath{~\mathrm{kW~}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{kg}^{-1}}$ , along with the maximum power density of $22\ensuremath{~\mathrm{kW~}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}}$ at 49.60 W h $\mathrm{kg^{-1}}$ (Figure 11e). Moreover, an ordered porous $\mathrm{Mn}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{N}$ -doped carbon/graphene (MCG) composite was synthesized by a simple carbonization of Mnbased MOFs (Mn-MOFs) using polystyrene (styrene-co-AA) as template.[158] The MCG displayed excellent electrochemical performances, including a specific capacitance of $456\ensuremath{\mathrm{~F~}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ at 1 A $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ and $246\mathrm{~F~g^{-1}}$ in an aqueous 1 m ${\mathrm{Na}}_{2}{\mathrm{SO}}_{4}$ electrolyte at $20\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ and good cyclic stability with $98.10\%$ of the initial capacitance remaining after 2000 cycles (Figure 11c). In addition, ruthenium oxide $(\mathrm{RuO}_{2})$ nanoparticles embedded in nanoporous carbon material (denoted as ${\mathrm{RuO}}_{2}/{\mathrm{PC}})$ was prepared that behaved excellent electrochemical properties in terms of high specific capacity of $539.60\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ at $1\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}$ and a capacitance retention of $81.50\%$ at 200 A $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ .[147] $\mathrm{MnO}_{x}–\mathrm{CS}$ nanocomposites, which have been reported previously in LIBs, also exhibited excellent performance for supercapacitors.[68]
## 3.3. Others
Many other supercapacitors were constructed to fulfill the energy demand as well. A hierarchical porous carbon displayed a higher specific capacitance when compared to some microporous carbons smaller than $1\mathrm{nm}$ .[159] Five MOF-derived porous carbons were prepared and occupied with DMF and NMP molecules.[160] After a series of electrochemical inspections, the activated porous carbon, which was referred to as APC-DMF, exhibited the best porous properties and largest specific capacitances $[153\mathrm{~F~g^{-1}}$ at $5~\mathrm{mV}~\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ and $156\mathrm{~F~g^{-1}}$ at $0.50\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}})$ . Furthermore, metal sulfide carbon composites also have wide applications.[161] $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}$ nanoparticles embedded in N, S-codoped carbon $(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}/\mathrm{NS}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{C})$ were synthesized to be an electrode material for SCs, and exhibited large specific capacity $(734\mathrm{F}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $1\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1})$ ), excellent rate performance $(653\mathrm{~F~g^{-1}}$ at $10\mathrm{~A~}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ ) and superior cyclic stability $(99.80\%$ capacitance retention after 140 000 cycles). More surprisingly, 2D porphyrin impeller framework-3 (PPF-3) MOF nanosheet was synthesized first, and after the simultaneous sulfidation and carbonization process, 2D CoSNC nanocomposites were obtained.[162] The obtained composites showed high rate capability, high capacitance, and long cycling performance. As observed from the galvanostatic charge/discharge curves in Figure 11d, the composites exhibited approximately symmetric shapes with small plateaus. This novel material delivered a much higher specific capacitance than the bulk composite electrode (as shown in Figure 11b).
## 3.4. Summary
The performances of MOF-derived carbon materials applied in SCs have been discussed in the aforementioned context, and we can conclude that $\mathrm{Co_{9}S_{8}/N S–C}$ , which is derived from a N-rich Co-MOF, displayed the best capacitance among various carbon materials. This may result from the high theoretical capacitance of $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}$ . The drawbacks of low conductivity and instability can be eliminated by the N, S-codoped optimal structure. Meanwhile, this unique structure can maintain the structural integrity of the MOF precursor and shorten the ion diffusion path.
## 4. Electrocatalytic Reactions
Though vast advancements have been made in the design and application of MOF-based materials, it was not until the very recently that their potential applications in the field of electrochemical catalysis drew the attention of researchers.[164–171] The growing world demand for energy supports the exploration and development of new materials for chemical energy conversion and storage.[172] In this way, fuel cells, particularly $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{O}_{2}$ or $\mathrm{H}_{2}/$ air batteries, have become promising candidates to provide energy for vehicles and convert $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ (with $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ or air) into energy produced by intermittent renewable sources such as sunlight, wind and waves.[173,174] OER is the core step of anodic reaction, where the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in the solvent is oxidized to $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , and ORR is the main reaction of cathode reaction, in which $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ is reduced to produce $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ via a $2^{-}$ electron pathway, or $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ through a $4^{-}$ electron pathway. Here, we focus on the application of MOF-based materials as catalysts in important energy-conversion electrochemical reactions, including the oxygen reduction reaction, oxygen evolution reaction and hydrogen evolution reaction.[175] The materials mentioned in the following text are collected in Table 3.
## 4.1. ORR
So far, carbon materials based on transition metal oxides have gained much attention in catalyst due to their attractive features of earth-abundant, low preparation cost, superior electrochemical stability and excellent conductivity.[197–199] In the earlier days, Hassan et  al. reported $\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{NiCo}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanocomposite fibers as promising ORR catalysts.[200] Benefiting from the incorporated carbon matrices, the composite showed an enhanced electrochemical performance. Figure 12B shows the Linear-sweep voltammetry (LSV) curves of different catalysts. It can be seen that both the half-wave potential $\left(E_{1/2}\right)$ and the initial potential of $\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{NiCo}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ (0.53 and 0.59 V) are more positive than others. Otherwise, another kind of metal oxide carbon material, which consisted of self-supporting nitrogen-doped carbon-loaded titanium nanofibers $\scriptstyle(\mathrm{N-TiO}_{2}@\mathrm{C})$ was also constructed to test ORR activities.[201] The $\mathrm{N-TiO}_{2}@\mathsf{C}{\cdot}0.75$ $\mathrm{0.75~g}$ additive cyanamide) exhibited not only better current density and onset potential than other comparable catalysts (as shown in Figure 12A), but remarkable methanol tolerance. Recently, with further step of exploring, nitrogen–graphene oxide $(\mathrm{N-GO})/\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanocrystals were designed and performed an onset potential of $0.91\mathrm{V}.^{[202]}$ Figure 12C demonstrates that the electrochemical stability of the $\mathrm{N-GO/Co_{3}O_{4}}$ catalyst is significantly enhanced when compared to commercial $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ catalyst. The cathodic current density shown in Figure 12D confirms a higher activity of $\Nu{-}\mathrm{GO}/\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ . More importantly, they for the first time proved that both the number and the dense location of catalytically active ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ cations and the electron density configuration around the ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ sites were essential for effective ORR.
However, the investigations of metal oxide carbon materials are still limited by the demerits of sluggish kinetics.[203,204] $Z\mathrm{n}$ -based MOFs were chosen for ORR, because they have high surface area and carbon content, and a large number of efforts have been devoted to $Z\mathrm{n}$ -based MOF-derived materials.[205] High surface area carbon derived from MOF-5 $(Z\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}})$ was prepared by in situ $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ .[176] When the pyrolysis temperature was increased to $900~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the nitrogen content decreased to $7\%$ , and the obtained material (noted as MOFCN-900) showed excellent ORR activity with initial potential of $35~\mathrm{mV}$ (vs $\mathrm{Hg/HgO)}$ in an alkaline medium, along with high durability and a strong disinclination to methanol crossing. Figure 13d shows the performance comparison of MOF-CN900 and $\mathrm{Pt/C40}$ . Moreover, Zhao et al.[178] introduced a brilliant solvent-assistant-linker-exchange strategy and a subsequent pore-expansion carbonization process to transform MOFs into foam-like carbons which possessed high surface area and hierarchical pores. ZIF-8, EZIF (triazole-based MOF designed in the article) and DMOF (triazole-based MOF prepared directly according to the previous study for comparison) were prepared for comparison, and the EZIF was found to have the best performance. The structure difference between ZIF-8 and EZIF is shown in Figure 13a, and the SEM image is located in Figure 13b. Interestingly, the catalytic carbon possessed micro-, meso- and macropores coexisting in a hierarchically porous structure. As a result, it showed superior ORR activities. LSV curves revealed that EZIF-C expressed a much more positive onset potential $(-30\ \mathrm{~V})$ and higher current density than ZIF-C (Figure 13e). In addition, $\mathrm{{Pt/Ni}}$ bimetallic nanoparticles dispersed on highly porous carbon obtained. The product gained at a pyrolysis temperature of $950~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (denoted as Pt-Ni/PC 950) displayed a prominent positive shift of $90~\mathrm{mV}$ in $\boldsymbol{E_{\mathrm{onset}}}$ in comparison to the commercial $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ $(20\%)$ .[50] Another carbon material with a hierarchically mesoporous structure was formed via the pyrolysis of an interpenetrated nonporous Zn-containing MOF.[183] As a result of the pore-forming function of the participating $Z\mathrm{n}$ ions, the final carbon showed a high ORR activity with initial and half wave potentials up to 1 and $0.90~\mathrm{V},$ respectively. Currently, a reliable and controllable route was described to prepare N–C.[184] The as-prepared N–C showed advantages of high activity, high durability, and methanol-tolerant as an efficient pH-universal electrocatalyst for ORR, showing comparable or even better activity as compared with the commercial $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ catalysts not only in alkaline media but also in acidic and neutral electrolyte. Figure 13c shows the SEM image of the N–C material, and rotating disk electrode (RDE) measurements are shown in Figure 13f. The N–C-1000 exhibited an initial potential of $1\mathrm{~V~}$ and a half wave potential $\left(E_{1/2}\right)$ of $0.90{\mathrm{~V~}}$ with a limiting current density of around $-5.90\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , manifesting a superior ORR activity.
Beside the $Z\mathrm{n}$ -containing MOFs, Co-based MOFs are also popular in the research community.[173,177,204] A unique “cagein-cage” cobalt-based MOF was chosen for a one-step pyrolysis procedure to obtain a nuclear shell $\mathsf{C o@C}$ structure with ${\approx}60\ \mathrm{wt}\%$ Co particles in the prepared sample.[206] The nanocomposites showed an excellent oxygen reduction reaction activity with a better durability and methanol tolerance compared to the commercial $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ catalyst (Figure 14e). Therefore, on condition that both $Z\mathrm{n}$ -MOFs and Co-MOFs perform well in electrochemical catalysis, their combination may deliver a better performance. Liu et al. studied a bimetallic Zn, Co-ZIF.[185] The preparation process is shown in Figure 14a. After electrochemical investigation, the material prepared at $900^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (ZCP-CFs-9) was identified as the best ORR catalyst in the series of samples. It displayed a more positive half-wave potential of $-135\ \mathrm{mV}$ (vs $\mathrm{\sfAg/AgCl)}$ (Figure 14d), as well as stronger tolerance and better methanol cross durability when compared with the commercial $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ catalyst. Similarly, 1D hierarchically porous N- and Co-doped carbon nanotubes were produced from Zn, Co-ZIF.[186] Figure 14b describes the specific formation procedure. Electrochemical characterization demonstrated its excellent electrocatalytic activity with a positive onset potential $(1~\mathrm{{V}_{R H E})}$ , positive half-wave potential $(0.87~\mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{RHE}})$ and excellent stability with a retention current of ${\approx}97\%$ over $24\mathrm{h}$ , which surpassed that of the $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ catalysts (as shown in Figure 14c). Moreover, taking advantage of the low cost and natural abundance, iron-containing MOFs are considered a good substitute to $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ and thus have been widely applied.[207–209] An iron/ nitrogen doped porous carbon material was gained through the modification of MOF-253 step by step.[187] The porous carbon material presented an equal onset potential $(980~\mathrm{~mV})$ and $20~\mathrm{mV}$ higher half-wave potential $(840~\mathrm{mV})$ than that of $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ $(20\mathrm{wt\%})$ under alkaline conditions, which revealed a great ORR activity. However, under acidic conditions, the initial potential and the half wave potential were found to be 20 and $30~\mathrm{mV}$ lower than $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ $(20\mathrm{wt\%})$ , respectively.
## 4.2. OER
In addition to the ORR, the OER is vital to catalysis as well.[169] As mentioned above, Co-based MOFs possess a wide application in many different fields because of their active transition metal chemical properties.[167] This is the same in OER catalysis. In 2014, strongly interacting $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and carbon were prepared to compose a hybrid porous nanowire arrays.[210] After carbonization, the resultant exhibit $52.10~\mathrm{wt\%}$ carbon content and a surface area of $251\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . Then the resultant was directly used as the working electrode for OER, and showed an initial potential of 1.47 V. In addition, $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanocrystals embedded in N-doped multiwalled carbon nanotube (noted as $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{N}.$ - MWCNTs) hybrids were prepared through the simple carbonization and oxidation procedure of MOFs.[182] The structure of the obtained material is shown in Figure 15f. A durability test of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{N}$ -MWCNTs was carried out by means of a chronopotentiometry measurement (Figure 15d). An initial potential of $1.50\mathrm{V},$ as well as an overpotential of $320\mathrm{mV},$ was achieved at $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ and remained stable over $25\mathrm{~h~}$ of testing. Further confirmed by the inset picture of Figure 15d, it can be concluded that the $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}@N\mathrm{\cdotMWCNT}}$ hybrids displayed a good durability in alkaline solutions. Another Co-MOF was used to prepare $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{CoS}@\mathrm{CoO@}\ @\mathrm{C$ nanoparticles via an annealing method.[188] The resultant exhibited an excellent OER activity with a lower overpotential $(150~\mathrm{mV})$ than $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ $(370~\mathrm{mV})$ and Pt NPs $(600~\mathrm{mV})$ , and exhibited the best OER activity after activation with a minimum overpotential of $28~\mathrm{mV}$ in a KOH aqueous solution. The sample also showed an obvious photo response to visible light irradiation and lowered onset potential, which was probably associated with the semiconducting characteristics of the spin-polarized CoO and the quantum confinement effect of the nanoparticles. Likewise, ${\mathrm{CoO}}_{{\mathrm{{x}}}}{\mathrm{{-MoC}}}$ heterostructures with nitrogen-doped carbon encapsulated in them were reported as high-performance oxygen evolution electrocatalysts.[189] The formation process is shown in Figure 15a. As a result of the addition of a Mo source, the hybrid can be tuned to form a nanowire-assembled porous microstructure (as shown in Figure 15e), leading to greatly improved oxygen evolution reaction activity. As shown in Figure 15c, the $\mathrm{CoO}_{x}{\mathrm{-MoC/NC-}}2$ , which possesses an atomic ratio of 21:1, showed the lowest overpotential of $330~\mathrm{mV}$ at $10\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{\cm}^{-2}$ , which is much smaller than other materials shown in the figure.
In another work, a dual-functional electrocatalyst with core–shell structure was reported by Wang et  al.[179] First, the ZIF-8 seeds were synthesized by the coordination of ${\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ with 2-methylimidazole, then ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ ions can easily fixed on the surface of ZIF-8 due to the coordination between ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ and 2-methylimidazole, and finally the $\underline{{{7}}}\mathrm{IF}{\cdot}8@\bigcirc\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}67$ core–shell structure was successfully formed. After carbonization treatment of ZIF- ${8\textcircled{\omega}}\mathrm{ZIF}{-67}$ , the high graphite carbon coated on nitrogen-doped carbon $(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{GC})$ was obtained. Benefitting from the synergetic effect between NC and GC in ${\mathsf{N C@G C}}$ structure, a lower potential of $1.57~\mathrm{V}$ was required to touch $10\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ when compared to commercial $\mathrm{Ir/C}$ (1.59 V). The strong interaction can remarkably improve the electron transport rate and reaction dynamics during the OER. Additionally, the poor conductivity and stability of amorphous carbon present in NC were circumvented by highly graphitized carbon derived from ZIF-67 in the structure, and due to the strong interaction between the highly graphitized carbon layer and
Co nanoparticles, the ${\mathsf{N C@G C}}$ showed a distinguished stability as well. Similarly utilizing ZIF-67 as the precursor, the ZIF-67/COF composite, which was mentioned in the former battery section, was prepared to test the OER activities.[65] In advantage of the synergistic effect of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and $_{\mathrm{N-C}}$ derived from the unique ZI $\operatorname{F}@\operatorname{COF}$ structure, the resulting ${\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}/$ N–C featured $330~\mathrm{\mV}$ overpotential at $10\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{\cm}^{-2}$ . DFT calculation was delivered to identify the catalytic mechanism of OER. According to the DFT result, these superior OER properties could be attributed to geometrical and electronic effects of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N-C}$ on activation and adsorption/desorption of reaction species.
Nevertheless, Qian et  al.[181] utilized an MOF containing Zn, N, and B as the precursor to synthesize double doped and nonmetallic porous carbon materials as bifunctional catalysts. As can be seen from Table 3, temperature is an important factor for catalytic reactions. The gradually increasing reaction temperature can promote exposure to active sites, while an excessive temperature will lead to the destruction of structure. The B, N-codoped porous carbon obtained at a pyrolysis temperature of $1100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (B,N–C-1100) showed the best catalytic performances when compared to the other two products (B, N–C-900, N–C-1000). During the OER tests, the highest $\boldsymbol{E_{\mathrm{onset}}}$ $(894~\mathrm{mV})$ and the best $E_{1/2}$ $(803\mathrm{mV})$ were displayed due to its high surface area and large content of pyridinic N. Moreover, due to the merits of their flexible chemical performance, transition metals have attracted further attention.[190] Carbon-­confined $\mathrm{Ni}_{2}\mathrm{P}$ nanocrystals supported on graphene were recently designed according to the scheme illustrated in Figure 15b. The resulting material showed excellent OER performance with a prominent overpotential of $285~\mathrm{mV}$ at $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ and outstanding durability.
## 4.3. HER
The efficient HER is an essential energy conversion process for sustainable development of a hydrogen-based society.[174] A lowtemperature phosphating process was designed to construct defect-rich CoP/nitrogen-doped carbon composites.[193] As a catalyst, the obtained CoP-N–C displayed high HER activities at $31~\mathrm{mV},$ a large exchange current density of $0.16~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and good stability. Meanwhile, Figure 16e demonstrates Tafel slopes of 118, 54, 49, and $42~\mathrm{mV}$ decade−1, while the Tafel slope for $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ is $34\mathrm{mV}$ decade−1. Another metal phosphide was prepared using a Cu-based MOF as a single precursor and template for the first time.[174] Figure 16b shows the specific procedure. The resulting $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{P}$ nanoparticles coated with a N, P-codoped carbon shell demonstrated remarkable activity for both the hydrogen evolution and oxygen reduction reactions. Tafel slopes of 98, 76, and $87~\mathrm{mV}$ decade−1 are displayed in Figure 16f for $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{P}@$ NPPC-600, 650, and 700, respectively, confirming that the phosphide did have a great effect on the HER process. Otherwise, a composite was synthesized via pyrolysis of a Co-MOF $@$ glucose polymer $(\mathrm{Co-MOFs}@\mathrm{GP})$ , accompanied by partial oxidation of Co nanoparticles.[211] The obtained ${\mathrm{Co-Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}@{\mathrm{C}}{\cdot}n$ was found to exhibit higher catalytic activity than those without oxidation during $\mathrm{NaBH}_{4}$ hydrolysis. Moreover, a novel strategy of in situ formation of Co/N-carbon is raised in Figure 16c.[194] The asprepared material exhibited a slight overpotential of $103\mathrm{mV}$ (vs RHE) at $10\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ while $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ $(20\mathrm{wt\%})$ needed an overpotential of $35~\mathrm{mV}$ (vs RHE) (as shown in Figure 16g), indicating a superior HER activity.
Taking the similar chemical properties of copper and zinc into account, a $\mathtt{C u}$ /nanoporous carbon composite $\mathrm{\Gamma(Cu/NPC}$ composite) was synthesized by directly carbonized from MOF199 without any carbon precursor additive.[212] Although high resistance to faradaic processes, the $\mathrm{Cu/NPC}$ composite showed favorable catalytic activity for the HER as a nonplatinum electrocatalyst. Analogously using MOF-199 as the template, Mandegarzad et  al.[195] proposed a novel catalyst based on Cu–Pd bimetallic nanoparticles supported on nanoporous carbon composites (NPCC) through a three-step process. Cu–Pd/NPCC derived from the MOF prepared by the electrochemical method exhibited an enhanced catalytic activity toward HER in $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ solution compared to that derived from the MOF prepared by the hydrothermal method. Moreover, a simple method was reported for synthesizing a hybrid of 6 nm $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ nanoparticles, decorated with porous graphite nitrogen-rich carbon matrix.[213] The mesoporous $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}@\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}$ showed $1.97\ \mathrm{eV}$ band gap and acted as an efficient photocatalyst for hydrogen evolution from water in visible light. In addition, to address the issue of metal nanoparticles corroding in acidic media and to promote catalytic reactions on the carbon surface, core–shell nanocomposites with metal nanoparticles encapsulated were designed and applied in the HER process. Wang et al.[196] prepared a zinc–iron embedded porous carbon decorated with Au nanoparticle $(\mathrm{Au}@Z\mathrm{n-Fe-C})$ as a bifunctional electrocatalyst. The formation procedure is illustrated in Figure 16a. In advantage of Au nanoparticles loading, the electron transfer activity of the Zn–Fe–C was greatly promoted. The polarization curves of the different samples are shown in Figure 16d. It is observed that $\mathrm{Au}@\mathrm{Zn-Fe-C}$ exhibited a higher positive initial potential $(-80~\mathrm{mV}$ vs RHE) than those of Au $(-225~\mathrm{mV})$ and Zn–Fe–C $(-292~\mathrm{mV},$ ), but it is still slightly more negative than the commercial $20\mathrm{wt\%}$ Pt/C $(-6\mathrm{mV})$ .
## 4.4. Summary
Owing to their special frame structure, large specific surface area and low coordination metal characteristics, carbon materials derived from MOF have been used as a heterogeneous catalyst in catalytic reactions. After summarizing the articles reported in the field of electrochemical catalysis in recent decades, we discovered that EZIF-C displayed the best ORR performance with initial potential of $-0.03\ \mathrm{mV}_{\mathrm{:}}$ $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{Co}\mathrm{S}_{@}$ ${\mathrm{CoO}}\ @{\mathrm{C}}$ derived from Co-based MOFs exhibited the best OER properties with initial potential of $1\ \mathrm{mV},$ and $\mathrm{Au}@Z\mathrm{n-Fe-C}$ (Au nanoparticle–decorated zinc–iron embedded porous carbons) had the best HER properties with initial potential of $-0.08\mathrm{mV}$ and overpotential of $-0.12\mathrm{mV}.$ In ORR, the excellent performance of EZIF-C may be ascribed to the optimal solvent-assistant-linker-exchange method, resulting in a triazole assistant which contains three nitrogen atoms. During the decomposition process of nitrogen-rich ligands (1H-1, 2, 3-triazole), tremendous gaseous mixtures, including $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ , NO, HCN, and $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ , were released to form hierarchically porous structures, therefore contributing to an enhanced catalytic activity. When discussing OER, the $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{CoS}@\mathrm{CoO@}@\mathrm{C$ nanoparticles exhibited the best OER activity with a minimum overpotential of $28~\mathrm{mV}$ in a $1\ \mathrm{~M~}\ \mathrm{KOH}$ aqueous solution. An enhanced OER performance and lower onset potential were also achieved with an obvious photo-response to visible light. Such outstanding results may be associated with the semiconducting characteristics of the spin-polarized CoO and the quantum confinement effect of the nanoparticles. As for HER performance, the best performance of the $\mathrm{Au}@\mathrm{Zn-Fe-C}$ nanocomposites may benefit from Zn–Fe–C conductive shells, as they provide a rapid electron transport space, and the encapsulating of Au nanoparticles which manufactures active sites for electrocatalytic reactions.
## 5. Water Treatments
Many efforts have been made to reduce and/or remove environmental pollutants, and a large number of environmental pollution treatment technologies have emerged, such as the biological treatment, chemical oxidation, adsorption and membrane separation methods, etc.[214] Among them, the adsorption method has always been favored. The advantages of high specific surface area, high and adjustable porosity, diversity of structural composition, open metal sites, and chemical modification allow MOF-derived carbon materials to have a great superiority in the application of adsorption, thanks to their increased active sites.[215] Table 4 summarizes some representative works in water treatment application.
## 5.1. MOF-C
In the early years, a porous ${\mathrm{C}}{\mathrm{-Fe}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ nanocomposite was synthesized from an iron-containing MOF as a magnetically separable and recyclable super adsorbent for the removal of environmental pollutants (oil/hydrocarbons and dye/phenols).[216] However, the industrial-level demand of super absorbents is still not satisfied. In the last several years, MOFderived carbon materials have made great progresses on water treatment. Fe precursor-encapsulated ZIF-8 was used recently for preparing Fe nanoparticles decorated nanoporous carbon (Fe/C).[219] Figure $17\mathrm{b}$ simulates the fabrication of the Fe/C composite. The nanocomposite possessed a superior absorption characteristic with a minimum reflection loss (RL) value of 29.50 dB at $17.20\:\mathrm{GHz}$ with a thickness of $2.50\ \mathrm{mm}$ (as shown in Figure 17d). Taking the similar chemical characteristics into consideration, and inspired from previous work, porous $\mathrm{Ni}/\mathrm{C}$ composites were gained from Ni-based MOFs, and exhibited a good RL value of 51.80 dB.[220] Likely using Ni-containing MOF, Jin et al.[222] prepared porous carbon/carbon nanotube hybrids encapsulating nickel nanoparticles (Ni/PC-CNT). On account of the high surface area $(999~\mathrm{~m}^{2}~\mathrm{~g}^{-1})$ , large pore volume $(0.90~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ and well-developed graphitized wall, the $\mathrm{\DeltaNi/}$ PC-CNT composites showed excellent adsorption capacities of ${\approx}898\ \mathrm{mg\g^{-1}}$ for malachite green (MG), $818\mathrm{mgg^{-1}}$ for Congo red (CR), $395\mathrm{\mg\g^{-1}}$ for rhodamine B (Rh B), $312\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for methylene blue (MB), and $271\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for methyl orange (MO) dyes.
With the continuous deepening of research, various materials were found to behave well in adsorption. An original 3D twofold Zn-based MOF $([Z\mathrm{nL}]\cdot5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ was designed and pyrolyzed by Zhang et al. to take up $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ .[217] The influence of temperature on material structure is also reflected in the treatment of aqueous solution (as shown in Table 4). When the temperature rises to a certain extent, the elements in the material are pyrolyzed or vaporized to form pores and thereby improve the surface area and adsorption capacity of the material. Porous carbons derived from MOFs (PCDMs; the MOF presented here is ZIF-8) were produced for the removal of ibuprofen and diclofenac from water.[221] Among the tested materials, the PCDM obtained under $1000^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (PCDM-1000) was found to perform best for both ibuprofen (IBP) and diclofenac (DCF), absorbing 3 and 5 times, respectively, as much as commercial activated carbon (as shown in Figure 17c). Later, porous carbons were further studied via introducing an ionic liquid (IL) to ZIF-8 to equip it with a much higher nitrogen content, and these displayed a superior absorption activity in both aqueous and nonaqueous media.[224] Furthermore, magnetic carbon sponge (MCS) was prepared using ZIF-67 to be the precursor.[218] This type of magnetic material was produced for removing floating oil from water and separating oil droplets from oil/water emulsions, as well as for some environmental catalysis and catalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production. Moreover, another approach to gain a high nitrogen-content carbon material was to use aminoguanidine hydrochloride (AG) as a high nitrogen content resource. The final product, mesoporous carbon nitrides (MCNs), was functionalized with highly dispersed chromium oxide nanoparticles by utilizing a unique MOF $(\mathrm{MIL}\cdot100(\mathrm{Cr}))$ as the template. The preparation procedure is clearly shown in Figure $17\mathrm{a}$ . The MCN was proven to display a superior high specific surface area, and therefore resulted in a large $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity of $16.80\ \mathrm{mmol\g^{-1}}$ , which was much higher than that of MCN materials reported before.
## 5.2. Other Techniques
With the development of industrial technology, more and more toxic substances are released into the aquatic environment.
For some substances difficult to remove, the detection and the control of content are particularly important. Arsenic (As) is considered to be one of the most toxic ions affecting human healthy, as it will cause many diseases including carcinogenic effects, heart attack, hypertension, nervous disorders, stillbirths, diabetes mellitus, emphysema, bronchitis, lymphoma, and so on.[225] Recent progress in efficient, sustainable and green method to solve environmental problems is photocatalysis because of its merits of nontoxicity, reproducibility and widerange functions.[226] For example, Shenashen et  al.[227] fabricated NiO sphere flowers for removal and recovery of ultratrace levels of arsenite $(\mathsf{A s O}_{3}^{3-})$ species. The NiO sphere consists of feathery or leafy surfaces (LSF), bushy sphere dendrites (BSD) of NiO/poly-carbon nitride (poly-CN) with interlinked branches and tree-like chains. In the merits of this complicated core– shell structure, the NiO sphere offered superior control of the diffusion of $\mathrm{AsO_{4}}^{3-}/\mathrm{AsO_{4}}^{3-}$ species and selective trapping and oxidation of As(III)-to-As(V) species in water. Considering the advantages of large surface area and porous structure of MOFs, which is in accordance with the excellent adsorption activity, Azzam et  al.[228] fabricated a mesoporous nanospherical necklaces (NSN), in which iron precursor was chelated with ethylenediaminetetraacetic acid (EDTA). The NSN showed a pretty high percentage of trapped $\operatorname{As}(\mathrm{V})$ and $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ species (as shown in Figure 18a) despite the low dose of solid NSN. Benefiting from the unique chelating structure, the NSN provided enormous active sites for adsorption, and $100\%$ adsorption uptake of these toxic ions focusing on $\mathrm{As(V)}$ and $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ . The adsorption capacities of $\operatorname{As}(\mathrm{V})$ and $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ species from adsorption isotherms in batch experiments were 306.70 and $406.50\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively.
Detection and quantification of zinc ions in water are also important and urgent problems to be solved, because the metabolism of free ${\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ ions is related to many pathological phenomena. Divalent zinc ions were found in many human tissues, and play crucial roles in human function. The presence of excess free ${\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ ions promotes a lot of harmful side effects and diseases, such as Alzheimer’s disease, ischemic stroke, epilepsy and infant diarrhea.[229] Fluorescence technique has emerged as an original approach for detecting different target ions because of its high simplicity and sensitivity. MOFs material can be used as a platform for manufacturing fluorescent chemosensors. El-Sewify et al.[230] fabricated a water-stable supermicroporous fluorescent chemosensors (MFCs) by directly decorate hydrophobic fluorescent probes into the $\mathrm{Zr}$ -based MOFs to determine the ultratraces $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ions. According to Figure 18b and the inset diagram, the MFCs achieved an LOD of $0.56~\mathrm{ppb}$ and wide-range determination from 0.50 to $2000\ \mathrm{ppb}$ during the binding of $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ion target. Moreover, they also analyzed the physical and chemical properties of the UiO-66 (a $\mathrm{Zr}$ -based MOF) and constructed a ratiometric fluorescent chemosensor (RFC) for the sensitive and selective detection of ultratraces of $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ions from water resources, Figure 18d displays a linear relationship with ultratrace concentrations of $\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}$ ions.[231] With the increasing amount of $\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}$ ion, the curve exhibited a significant enhancement in fluorescence spectra, and confirmed by the inset diagram of Figure 18d, the RFC finally realized a limit of detection (LOD) of $0.16\ \mathrm{ppb}$ . Furthermore, after further unremitting efforts, they expanded the detection range, replaced hydrophobic fluorescent probes with rhodamine ethylene-diamine salicylaldehyde to detect ${\mathrm{Bi}^{3+}}$ ions, and achieved an LOD of 0.16 (Figure 18c), proving the universality of method.[232]
## 5.3. Summary
MOF-derived carbon materials exhibit broad application prospects in the field of selective adsorption. After much experimentation and literature review, we regard porous carbon derived from ZIF-8 as the best adsorbent for water treatment. The PCDM-1000 was found to perform best in absorbing both IBP and DCF, 3 and 5 times more absorbing activities than activated carbon. This wonderful adsorption activity may owe to the remarkable BET surface area of $1855~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . Moreover, the PCDM-1000 can act as a H-bond donor because of the special H-bonding, $\pi{-}\pi$ bonding and hydrophobicity, while the IBP or DCF can be the H-bond acceptor. Meanwhile, carbonization temperature also plays an important role in the adsorption reaction.
## 6. Others
The excellent electrochemical properties of MOF-derived carbon materials make it a great material in sensing.[233–235] For instance, due to their abilities for selective absorbing singlestranded DNA (ssDNA) and quenching the labeled fluorescent dye,[236,237] carbon materials were claimed to be sensing platforms for DNA detection.[238,239] A magnetic porous carbon (MPC) nanocomposite produced by a one-pot thermolysis of iron-containing MOFs (MIL-88A) was reported recently to behave well in forming double-stranded DNA with target DNA and in releasing ssDNA probes from the MPC surface.[240] The composite exhibited high sensitivities with a detection limit of $1\times10^{-9}$ m and excellent selectivity to specific target DNA. Another experiment utilizing IR-MOF-8 as the precursor was conducted to detect the anesthetic lidocaine (LID).[241] After considerable effort, the researchers finally succeeded in constructing a hybrid electrochemical sensor and achieved an outstanding linear range of $0.20\times10^{-12}$ m to $8\times10^{-9}\mathrm{~M~}$ , along with a remarkable $6.70\times10^{-14}\mathrm{~N~}$ m detection limit (at an $\mathrm{{S/N}}$ ratio of 3). What’s more, Li et al.[242] delivered a highly efficient profiling of N-linked glycans through pyrolysis of ${\mathrm{Zn}}{\mathrm{-MOF}}$ for removal of zinc. After further handling, the final sample served for the selective isolation of N-linked glycans from complex biological samples. Twenty-six N-linked glycans could be identified from the digest of a standard glycoprotein ovalbumin at a concentration of $0.01\upmu\mathrm{g}\upmu\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ , and the detection limit of glycans could be as low as $1\mathrm{ng}\upmu\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ , with 21 N-linked glycans identified.
## 7. Conclusions and Perspectives
The development of advanced materials is of great significance due to the current demand for high-performance energy storage and conversion applications. Recent advances in MOFrelated materials have shown that the utilization of MOF-based materials as precursors or sacrificial templates is a powerful means to prepare new materials with unique structures. So far, many MOF derivative materials have been successfully prepared and used in a wide range of applications. MOFderived carbon materials with high porosity and uniform pore size distribution can bring with rapid electron/mass transfer rates. Porous metal compounds derived from MOFs can greatly eliminate volume expansion and comminution problems. Compared with traditional inorganic porous materials, MOFs have larger specific surface areas, higher porosity, and more flexible structures and functions. Carbon materials are well known for their electrical conductivity, and MOF-derived carbon materials combine the advantages of both. The emergence of novel materials has greatly promoted the mutual development of various disciplines. In this review, we have summarized the various applications of MOF-derived carbon materials, including batteries, supercapacitors, electrocatalysts and water treatments. Their synthetic processes, material structures, and corresponding chemical performances are described in detail, indicating that MOF-derived carbon materials are very promising in many fields with good performances. However, some challenges remain to be solved. Here, we present our perspectives on some key problems in this field.
1)	 The preparation route of most MOF-derived carbon materials is depended on high-temperature calcination. However, calcination usually destroys the ordered structure and porous morphology of MOFs, and even leads to polymerization, which weakens the catalytic activity. Therefore, a low reaction temperature is generally preferred.   
2)	 Most of the studies on MOF-derived carbon materials are still focused on the preparation and characterization, but the research on their mechanisms in reactions is not very mature. A thorough understanding and mastery of the interfacial interactions between MOFs and the adscititious components are greatly in need since they could provide guidance to optimize the synthetic process of MOF-derived carbon materials for better performance.   
3)	 Although some strategies have been recommended to enhance the firmness between the MOF film and the carrier, such as spin-on polymer[232] adhesives or alkyl functionalized carriers, they do not have commercial value. Therefore, a universal, low cost, and easy operating strategy is needed.
To enhance the electrochemical usage of MOF-derived carbon materials, and therefore to better meet the industrial needs, we propose the following solutions:
1)	 Considering the merits of MOFs, the performance of MOFderived carbon materials can be further improved by regulating the structure, composition, and morphology of MOFs.   
2)	 Combining experimentation with theoretical computation is a powerful impetus for experimentation itself and will make
the theory completer and more sufficient. At the same time, the computational simulation methods can provide guidance for the applications prediction of new materials.
3)	 MOFs with exclusively designed features should be designed to achieve precise control of the structure, function, and performance of the MOF-derived materials.
MOF-derived carbon materials have been widely reported in last decade. Due to different testing environments, the collaboration of substances, and various elements, the carbon materials showed complex and varied chemical characteristics. Each component has an indispensable position and/or plays an important role in these materials, which can complement each other’s defects and even generate a synergistic effect to enhance corresponding performance. Generally, MOF-derived carbon materials always possess hierarchically porous structures, which are convenient for the electron conductivity, in that they provide a way for electrons to move and shorten the length of transfer channel. By controlling the reaction temperature, the structural stability was strengthened. Due to their promising features, MOF-derived carbon materials have been intensively studied in many fields, including batteries, supercapacitors, electrocatalytic reactions, and water treatments. Although numerous impressive advancements have been achieved, their electrochemical performances still need to be further improved. We expect that this review will provide a summary of the previous work to encourage a new breakthrough in the further development of MOF-derived carbon materials.
